<div style="background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:10px;padding:1.4rem 1.6rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
<h3 style="margin-top:0;color:var(--accent);">&#128218; What is this &amp; why does it exist?</h3>
<p>The Canvas class loads a DSL JSON definition, instantiates components, schedules them topologically, and executes them with variable resolution and history tracking. Without Canvas, workflows would be raw imperative code—impossible to serialize, introspect, version, or audit. — Replace with 2-4 plain-English sentences explaining what this sub-module does and why it exists. No jargon. Target audience: someone who hasn't read the code.</p>
<details style="margin-top:.8rem;">
<summary style="cursor:pointer;color:var(--accent2);font-weight:600;">&#128161; Real-world analogy</summary>
<p style="margin-top:.5rem;padding-left:1rem;border-left:3px solid var(--accent3);color:var(--text-muted);">Like a Python interpreter: canvas.py = bytecode loader + VM runtime; DSL JSON = bytecode; components = function objects; variables = stack frames; execution history = execution trace for debugging. — Replace with a vivid, concrete metaphor mapping this sub-module's core concept to everyday life (restaurant, airport, library, highway, etc.). 3-5 sentences.</p>
</details>
</div>

<!-- DESIGN RATIONALE — why this component is designed this way -->
<div style="background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:10px;padding:1.4rem 1.6rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
<h3 style="margin-top:0;color:var(--accent);">&#129300; Why Is It Designed This Way?</h3>
<p>Canvas uses lazy variable resolution (resolve at runtime, not parse-time) for flexibility with non-deterministic LLM outputs. Single-threaded execution avoids races. Iteration nodes re-run sub-DAGs with updated scopes, preserving semantics. Immutable history enables perfect audit trails for debugging and compliance. — 150+ words explaining the design choices: what pattern does this follow and why? What constraints shaped the implementation? What trade-offs were made and what was sacrificed?</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Design Choice</th><th>What Was Chosen</th><th>What Was Rejected</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>Variable resolution timing</td><td>Lazy (at runtime)</td><td>Eager (at load-time)</td><td>Lazy enables maximum flexibility for non-deterministic LLM outputs; eager would require re-compilation on every variable change</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Execution model</td><td>Single-threaded greedy traversal</td><td>Multi-threaded or async parallel execution</td><td>Single-threaded avoids race conditions and data corruption; simplicity is worth the latency cost for I/O-bound workflows</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Iteration scope isolation</td><td>Dynamic variable rebinding per iteration</td><td>Explicit scope stack with formal isolation</td><td>Dynamic rebinding is simpler but fragile; formal scope stack would add complexity. Current impl works but needs testing.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>History persistence</td><td>Immutable list appended during execution</td><td>On-demand query of execution state</td><td>Immutable history is perfect for audit trails and debugging; on-demand would lose information and add query latency</td></tr>
  <!-- Add 2-4 rows -->
</table>
</div>

