<!-- EXECUTION ALGORITHM DEEP-DIVE -->
<h2 id="execution-algorithm">Execution Algorithm Details</h2>
<p>The Canvas.execute() method follows a specific algorithm to schedule and run components. Understanding this is critical for debugging complex workflows.</p>

<h3>Topological Scheduling Algorithm</h3>
<p>The topological sort ensures that components with dependencies execute only after their dependencies complete. This is implemented using Kahn's algorithm (iterative BFS-based):</p>
<ol style="line-height:1.8;">
  <li><strong>Compute in-degrees:</strong> For each node, count how many edges point to it (incoming dependencies).</li>
  <li><strong>Initialize queue:</strong> Add all nodes with in-degree 0 to a queue. These have no dependencies and can run immediately.</li>
  <li><strong>Process queue:</strong> While queue is not empty:
    <ul style="margin-top:.5rem;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
      <li>Dequeue node N</li>
      <li>Add N to sorted order</li>
      <li>For each outgoing edge from N: decrement destination node's in-degree. If in-degree becomes 0, enqueue destination.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Detect cycles:</strong> If sorted order length < graph size, a cycle exists. Raise ValidationError.</li>
  <li><strong>Time complexity:</strong> O(V + E) where V = components, E = edges. Linear in graph size; very fast even for large workflows.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Greedy Execution Model</h3>
<p>After topological sort, Canvas uses a greedy single-threaded execution model:</p>
<ul style="line-height:1.8;">
  <li>Nodes are processed in topological order</li>
  <li>For each node: check if all dependencies have completed (in_degree == 0)</li>
  <li>If yes, call component.run(); store result in _outputs[node_id]</li>
  <li>If no, skip (will retry in next pass)</li>
  <li>Record execution step in history: {node_id, inputs, outputs, error, timestamp, duration_ms}</li>
  <li>On error: apply retry logic (exponential backoff, max 5 attempts), then exception_default_value or exception_goto</li>
  <li>Continue until all nodes executed or error unrecoverable</li>
</ul>

<h3>Variable Resolution Trace Example</h3>
<p>When a component parameter contains a variable reference (e.g., "search_query": "{{llm_1@output.text}}"), Canvas resolves it at runtime:</p>
<pre><code style="display:block;background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;padding:1rem;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;">1. Parameter value: "llm_1@output.text"
2. Split on @: component_id="llm_1", path="output.text"
3. Look up llm_1 in _outputs: {output: {text: "Hello", choices: [...]}}
4. Traverse path "output.text": _outputs["llm_1"]["output"]["text"]
5. Result: "Hello" → pass to component parameter
6. Component receives concrete value, not template</code></pre>

<p>This lazy evaluation allows workflows to handle non-deterministic LLM outputs without requiring re-compilation or DAG reconstruction.</p>

<!-- PERFORMANCE ANALYSIS -->
<h2 id="performance">Performance Characteristics</h2>
<p>The Canvas execution engine is optimized for real-world workflows with variable-length DAGs. Key performance considerations:</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Characteristics</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Topological Sort</strong></td><td>O(V + E) where V = components, E = edges</td><td>Single pass; reused across multiple runs if DAG unchanged</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Variable Resolution</strong></td><td>O(D × S) where D = path depth, S = scope stack depth</td><td>Lazy evaluation on first access; results cached in component outputs</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Node Execution</strong></td><td>Sequential; greedy when dependencies ready</td><td>No multi-threading; bottleneck is slowest component in critical path</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>History Storage</strong></td><td>O(N) memory where N = total executed nodes</td><td>Stored in _history list; enable only if needed for debugging/audit</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Iteration Scaling</strong></td><td>Sub-DAG cloning: O(T × |DAG|) where T = list item count</td><td>Each iteration wraps sub-DAG in local scope; components re-instantiated</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Error Recovery</strong></td><td>Propagation is O(V) worst case; retry backoff exponential</td><td>Retry overhead: ~100ms base, then 2x per attempt. Max 5 retries standard.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Hot Paths & Optimization</h3>
<ul style="line-height:1.8;">
  <li><strong>Execute Phase (60-80%):</strong> Component run() methods dominate. If slow, profile component logic (LLM timeouts, API latency, loop iterations).</li>
  <li><strong>Variable Resolution (10-20%):</strong> Deep scopes (iterations inside loops) are costlier. Cache variable references if accessed repeatedly.</li>
  <li><strong>Topological Sort (1-5%):</strong> Only on load(); negligible for re-execution. Use cached DAG for production workflows.</li>
  <li><strong>Retry Logic:</strong> Exponential backoff can add seconds per failure. For flaky tools, raise timeouts or add circuit breaker logic.</li>
</ul>

